(1) N+1 / 2N sequential query problem: one `findFirst` + one `findMany` per id, each `await`ed in the loop.

(2) Cost is ~2N DB round-trips in series. Latency grows linearly with |routineIds|; under load connection pool and RTT dominate, so throughput collapses as N grows.

(3) Bound to two queries, then join in memory:

```ts
async function loadRoutineCards(routineIds: string[]) {
  if (!routineIds.length) return [];
  const routineRows = await db.query.routines.findMany({
    where: inArray(routines.id, routineIds),
  });
  const runRows = await db.query.runs.findMany({
    where: inArray(runs.routineId, routineIds),
    // orderBy createdAt desc if needed; trim to 5 per id below
  });
  const runsByRoutine = new Map<string, typeof runRows>();
  for (const r of runRows) {
    const list = runsByRoutine.get(r.routineId) ?? [];
    if (list.length < 5) list.push(r);
    runsByRoutine.set(r.routineId, list);
  }
  return routineRows.map((routine) => ({
    routine,
    runs: runsByRoutine.get(routine.id) ?? [],
  }));
}
```

(Or one query with a relational `with: { runs: { limit: 5 } }` if the ORM supports it.)
